    <!-- "Knowledge base" — T-16 part A (tepera-dev-spec.md), real content. Part B (the
         doomscrolling/dopamine wiki) stays blocked on the owner's decision — deliberately
         absent here. -->
    <string name="settings_knowledge_base_action">Knowledge base</string>
    <string name="knowledge_base_screen_title">Knowledge base</string>
    <string name="knowledge_base_intro">A short explanation of how Tepera counts time and why it\'s built this way.</string>
    <string name="knowledge_base_section_1_title">Where the data comes from</string>
    <string name="knowledge_base_section_1_body">Online time and the day structure are counted through Android\'s system "Usage access" permission (Settings → Special access, not a pop-up dialog). The permission only grants access to app names and the moments they move to the foreground or background. Without it, manual activity logging keeps working fully — Tepera just won\'t have automatic Online time.\n\nTepera does not see screen content: not message text, not what you\'re actually reading or watching, not scrolling inside an app — a minute of reading looks the same as a minute of scrolling a feed. All data stays on the phone; the only network request is the "Suggest a feature" button, which opens the browser, not Tepera itself.</string>
    <string name="knowledge_base_section_2_title">Pauses and gates</string>
    <string name="knowledge_base_section_2_body">When enough time passes between uses of the phone, Tepera offers to label that gap with a category — that\'s the pause card on Home. The threshold is set by three levels (Less often / Normal / More often) in Settings, with no need to understand the algorithm\'s numbers. Labeling is a suggestion, not a tracking log — a pause you leave unlabeled is never recorded as a metric.\n\nGates (Settings → Apps with a delay) are a manual feature: you choose the app and the wait time yourself. Android doesn\'t let apps actually block another app from launching, so gates work through a pinned shortcut with a pause before launch — the original app icon stays in place and still opens it instantly. This is a deliberate delay you set for yourself, not a technical barrier — turning gates off for the day is one tap, no explanation needed.</string>
    <string name="knowledge_base_section_3_title">Sleep window</string>
    <string name="knowledge_base_section_3_body">The sleep window (Settings → Time and day boundaries) doesn\'t show sleep as an activity and says nothing about how much or how well you slept. It\'s only a time range excluded from the calculation: minutes inside the sleep window don\'t count toward the day structure, don\'t become pauses, and don\'t count toward the unlock counter.</string>
    <string name="knowledge_base_section_4_title">Why there are no notifications, streaks, or scores</string>
    <string name="knowledge_base_section_4_body">Tepera doesn\'t send notifications trying to pull you back, doesn\'t count day streaks, and doesn\'t award points or badges for usage. The numbers on screen are facts about time that has already passed, not a goal to reach — that\'s why the app never says "goal," only "reference," and colors never turn "alarming" no matter how much time has passed. Comparisons are only ever against your own history, never against other people\'s averages.</string>
