    <!-- T-16 B: what is known / not known about scrolling -->
    <string name="scrolling_notes_row">What is known about scrolling and dopamine</string>
    <string name="scrolling_notes_title">What is and isn\'t known</string>
    <string name="scrolling_notes_intro">This is not advice and not an assessment. It is only what science knows right now, and where scientists disagree with each other.</string>
    <string name="scrolling_notes_1_title">What this is about</string>
    <string name="scrolling_notes_1_body">Scrolling means moving through a feed with no goal set in advance. Tepera can\'t see what happens inside an app: a minute of reading and a minute of scrolling look the same to it.</string>
    <string name="scrolling_notes_2_title">What is known</string>
    <string name="scrolling_notes_2_body">Neuroscientists usually describe dopamine as part of a system tied to anticipating rewards and to learning, not as a \"pleasure hormone\". Feeds that keep refreshing are built so that the next item is unknown in advance — that is a described property of the design, not a verdict on a person.</string>
    <string name="scrolling_notes_3_title">What is not known</string>
    <string name="scrolling_notes_3_body">There is no single answer on whether regular social media use can be called an addiction. There is also no agreement on how strongly screen time is linked to wellbeing: some researchers see a noticeable link, others a very weak one, and others think the direction of the link is not established.</string>
    <string name="scrolling_notes_4_title">Where scientists disagree</string>
    <string name="scrolling_notes_4_body">Whether \"social media addiction\" is a separate condition. Whether screen time changes wellbeing, or people with a certain wellbeing spend more time in apps. Whether the time itself matters, or what a person does during it.</string>
    <string name="scrolling_notes_5_title">How this relates to Tepera</string>
    <string name="scrolling_notes_5_body">Tepera draws no conclusions about you from these questions. It only shows how you use your phone; what to do with that is up to you.</string>
